1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,614
Hello again, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,614 --> 00:00:16,272
Tonight we are travelling a very long way, both in distance and in time.

3
00:00:16,272 --> 00:00:21,300
But there is no need to hurry, and nothing you need to remember.

4
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:29,079
Just listen for as long as you like, and let the story drift on without you whenever you are ready.

5
00:00:30,379 --> 00:00:32,419
Make yourself comfortable.

6
00:00:32,419 --> 00:00:36,812
Feel the weight of your body settling down into the bed.

7
00:00:36,812 --> 00:00:38,851
Let your hands grow heavy.

8
00:00:38,851 --> 00:00:40,734
Let your shoulders drop.

9
00:00:40,734 --> 00:00:48,108
And breathe out slowly, the way warm air leaves a room when a window is opened in the evening.

10
00:00:49,408 --> 00:00:57,357
Tonight we are going to ancient Egypt, more than three thousand years ago, to the banks of the river Nile.

11
00:00:57,357 --> 00:01:04,482
We will spend a long, warm evening there, beside the water, among the palm trees and the reeds.

12
00:01:04,482 --> 00:01:16,181
We will visit a small village of craftsmen, share a supper on a flat roof under the stars, and listen to the old stories the Egyptians told about the night.

13
00:01:17,481 --> 00:01:19,735
It is the end of a hot day.

14
00:01:19,735 --> 00:01:23,491
The sun is sinking toward the western cliffs.

15
00:01:23,491 --> 00:01:27,998
And the air above the river is just beginning to cool.

16
00:01:37,298 --> 00:01:42,800
The Nile is wide here, and slow, and the colour of the evening sky.

17
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,235
On both sides of it there is a narrow ribbon of green.

18
00:01:47,235 --> 00:01:54,626
Fields of wheat and barley, groves of date palms, gardens of onions and lettuce and beans.

19
00:01:54,626 --> 00:02:03,495
And beyond that green ribbon, on both sides, the desert begins, sudden and golden, rising toward low cliffs.

20
00:02:04,796 --> 00:02:07,916
The ancient Egyptians had names for this.

21
00:02:07,916 --> 00:02:13,930
The black land, for the dark, rich soil along the river, where everything grew.

22
00:02:13,930 --> 00:02:19,030
And the red land, for the desert beyond it, dry and empty and vast.

23
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:27,707
Their whole world was this long, thin garden, running through the middle of the desert, fed entirely by the river.

24
00:02:29,008 --> 00:02:34,014
Along the bank, tall papyrus reeds grow thick in the shallow water.

25
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:40,440
Their stems are smooth and green, with feathery heads that sway in the evening breeze.

26
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:44,102
The Egyptians used papyrus for almost everything.

27
00:02:44,102 --> 00:02:57,104
They wove it into baskets and mats and sandals, tied it into bundles to make light boats, and pressed thin strips of it together to make the smooth pale sheets they wrote on.

28
00:02:58,403 --> 00:03:04,033
A small wooden boat glides past, its tall sail catching the last of the wind.

29
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:10,832
On the Nile, the wind usually blows from the north, while the current flows toward the north.

30
00:03:10,832 --> 00:03:22,309
So a boat going south can raise its sail and let the wind carry it upstream, and a boat going north can simply lower the sail, and drift home on the current.

31
00:03:22,309 --> 00:03:29,401
The river and the wind together, working in opposite directions, like a slow and patient machine.

32
00:03:30,701 --> 00:03:34,917
On the far bank, a farmer is finishing his work at a shaduf.

33
00:03:34,917 --> 00:03:42,085
It is a long wooden pole balanced on a post, with a bucket at one end and a heavy weight at the other.

34
00:03:42,085 --> 00:03:52,135
He dips the bucket into the river, and the weight lifts it gently up, so he can swing it round and pour the water into a channel for his field.

35
00:03:52,135 --> 00:03:53,751
Dip, lift, swing, pour.

36
00:03:53,751 --> 00:03:55,367
Dip, lift, swing, pour.

37
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:59,373
Then he sets it down, and wipes his face, and walks home.

38
00:04:00,673 --> 00:04:08,719
The sun touches the cliffs on the western horizon, and for a moment the whole river turns to gold.

39
00:04:08,719 --> 00:04:13,974
Then the light fades, quickly, the way it does in hot countries.

40
00:04:13,974 --> 00:04:19,968
The sky above the desert turns rose, then violet, then a deep, soft blue.

41
00:04:19,968 --> 00:04:22,924
And the first stars begin to appear.

42
00:04:32,223 --> 00:04:39,973
A little way from the river, in a quiet valley in the western hills, there is a small walled village.

43
00:04:39,973 --> 00:04:42,966
The people who live here are craftsmen.

44
00:04:42,966 --> 00:04:47,493
Stonecutters, plasterers, painters, sculptors, and scribes.

45
00:04:47,493 --> 00:04:56,010
Their work is to build and decorate the tombs of the kings, cut deep into the cliffs of a hidden valley nearby.

46
00:04:57,310 --> 00:05:05,250
Archaeologists call this village Deir el Medina, and it is one of the best known places in all of ancient Egypt.

47
00:05:05,250 --> 00:05:15,601
Because the people here could read and write unusually well, they left behind thousands of notes, scribbled on flakes of stone and broken pottery.

48
00:05:15,601 --> 00:05:16,664
Shopping lists.

49
00:05:16,664 --> 00:05:17,231
Letters.

50
00:05:17,231 --> 00:05:20,422
Records of who was absent from work, and why.

51
00:05:20,422 --> 00:05:23,824
One man stayed home because his wife was unwell.

52
00:05:23,824 --> 00:05:26,377
Another because he was brewing beer.

53
00:05:26,377 --> 00:05:29,779
Another because he had been stung by a scorpion.

54
00:05:31,079 --> 00:05:34,974
The houses stand close together along one main street.

55
00:05:34,974 --> 00:05:39,014
They are made of mud brick, plastered and painted white.

56
00:05:39,014 --> 00:05:43,053
Each has a few small rooms in a row, one behind another.

57
00:05:43,053 --> 00:05:47,164
A front room with a little shrine for the household gods.

58
00:05:47,164 --> 00:05:51,853
A main room with a raised bench and a column holding up the roof.

59
00:05:51,853 --> 00:05:57,623
A small kitchen at the back, open to the sky, with a clay oven for baking bread.

60
00:05:57,623 --> 00:06:00,797
And a staircase leading up to the flat roof.

61
00:06:02,097 --> 00:06:05,967
The workers did not go to the royal tombs every day.

62
00:06:05,967 --> 00:06:10,135
They worked in shifts, and their week was ten days long.

63
00:06:10,135 --> 00:06:21,968
They would spend their working days in the valley, sometimes sleeping in simple huts up on the ridge, and then come home to the village for their days of rest.

64
00:06:21,968 --> 00:06:25,094
Tonight, some of them have just come home.

65
00:06:26,394 --> 00:06:35,945
In the street, a few children are playing a game with small stones, under the watchful eye of a grandmother sitting in a doorway.

66
00:06:35,945 --> 00:06:39,499
A man carries a bundle of reeds on his shoulder.

67
00:06:39,499 --> 00:06:43,496
A cat slips along the top of a wall, silent and sleek.

68
00:06:43,496 --> 00:06:53,935
The Egyptians loved cats, and kept them in their homes, partly for company, and partly to keep away mice and snakes from the stores of grain.

69
00:06:55,235 --> 00:06:59,425
From inside the houses come the sounds of evening.

70
00:06:59,425 --> 00:07:06,463
The scrape of a grinding stone, as the last of the day's grain is turned into flour.

71
00:07:06,463 --> 00:07:08,223
The clink of pottery.

72
00:07:08,223 --> 00:07:09,144
Low voices.

73
00:07:09,144 --> 00:07:12,245
A baby fussing, and then going quiet.

74
00:07:12,245 --> 00:07:19,534
And drifting over everything, the warm, comforting smell of bread baking in clay ovens.

75
00:07:28,834 --> 00:07:34,909
Let us climb the narrow staircase of one of these houses, up onto the flat roof.

76
00:07:34,909 --> 00:07:40,073
In the hot months, the roof was the best place to be in the evening.

77
00:07:40,073 --> 00:07:44,705
It caught the breeze, and it was cooler than the rooms below.

78
00:07:44,705 --> 00:07:51,767
Families ate up here, and talked, and very often, they slept up here too, under the open sky.

79
00:07:53,067 --> 00:07:57,926
The roof is covered in mats woven from reeds and palm leaves.

80
00:07:57,926 --> 00:08:02,624
In one corner, some baskets of dates and onions are drying.

81
00:08:02,624 --> 00:08:09,872
In another, there is a low shelter made of palm fronds, to keep off the sun during the day.

82
00:08:09,872 --> 00:08:19,030
Now, in the evening, everyone sits out in the open, on the mats, with a few small oil lamps flickering beside them.

83
00:08:20,330 --> 00:08:22,341
Supper is simple, and good.

84
00:08:22,341 --> 00:08:25,170
Fresh bread, still warm from the oven.

85
00:08:25,170 --> 00:08:26,436
Onions and leeks.

86
00:08:26,436 --> 00:08:27,777
Beans and lentils.

87
00:08:27,777 --> 00:08:29,936
A handful of dates, and figs.

88
00:08:29,936 --> 00:08:33,883
Perhaps some fish from the river, or a little cheese.

89
00:08:33,883 --> 00:08:39,245
And to drink, beer, thick and mild and slightly sweet, made from barley.

90
00:08:39,245 --> 00:08:44,159
For the Egyptians, bread and beer were the most basic food of all.

91
00:08:44,159 --> 00:08:48,106
Workers were even paid partly in grain, to make them.

92
00:08:49,406 --> 00:08:56,054
The lamps are small dishes of clay, filled with oil, with a little twisted wick of linen.

93
00:08:56,054 --> 00:09:01,881
Their light is soft and golden, and it makes the faces around the circle glow.

94
00:09:01,881 --> 00:09:13,385
Beyond the edge of the roof, the village is dark, except for other small lights on other roofs, scattered like stars that have settled down for the night.

95
00:09:14,685 --> 00:09:16,472
Someone tells a story.

96
00:09:16,472 --> 00:09:18,097
Someone else laughs.

97
00:09:18,097 --> 00:09:27,765
A child, too tired to stay awake, curls up against her mother and falls asleep with a piece of bread still in her hand.

98
00:09:27,765 --> 00:09:34,184
Above them all, the sky is growing very dark, and very deep, and full of stars.

99
00:09:43,484 --> 00:09:47,544
The Egyptians watched the night sky with great care.

100
00:09:47,544 --> 00:09:53,166
And there was one star that mattered to them more than almost any other.

101
00:09:53,166 --> 00:09:54,572
We call it Sirius.

102
00:09:54,572 --> 00:09:58,320
It is the brightest star in the whole night sky.

103
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:00,038
They called it Sopdet.

104
00:10:01,338 --> 00:10:07,971
For part of every year, Sirius cannot be seen at all, because it is too close to the sun.

105
00:10:07,971 --> 00:10:16,468
But then, one morning in early summer, it appears again, for the first time, low in the east, just before sunrise.

106
00:10:16,468 --> 00:10:23,325
The Egyptians noticed that this happened at about the same time that the Nile began to rise.

107
00:10:23,325 --> 00:10:27,200
And so the return of that bright star became a sign.

108
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:28,691
The flood is coming.

109
00:10:28,691 --> 00:10:30,480
A new year is beginning.

110
00:10:31,779 --> 00:10:34,019
Every year, the Nile flooded.

111
00:10:34,019 --> 00:10:43,210
Rains far away to the south swelled the river, and slowly, over weeks, the water rose and spread out across the fields.

112
00:10:43,210 --> 00:10:49,002
When it went down again, it left behind a fresh layer of dark, fertile mud.

113
00:10:49,002 --> 00:10:52,709
That mud was the gift that made everything grow.

114
00:10:52,709 --> 00:10:57,806
Without the flood, there would have been no harvest, and no Egypt.

115
00:10:59,106 --> 00:11:06,470
The Egyptians divided their year into three seasons, each of four months, all shaped by the river.

116
00:11:06,470 --> 00:11:10,752
The season of the flood, when the fields lay under water.

117
00:11:10,752 --> 00:11:16,388
The season of growing, when the water went down and the seeds were planted.

118
00:11:16,388 --> 00:11:23,000
And the season of harvest, when the crops were gathered in, before the river rose again.

119
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:28,943
During the flood season, farmers could not work their fields.

120
00:11:28,943 --> 00:11:36,403
So many of them went to work on great building projects instead, or rested, or mended their tools.

121
00:11:36,403 --> 00:11:49,345
Imagine the whole valley turned into a shining lake, with villages standing on little islands of higher ground, and boats moving slowly between them in the evening light.

122
00:11:50,645 --> 00:11:53,252
Tonight, the river is low and calm.

123
00:11:53,252 --> 00:11:57,946
It is the season of harvest, and the fields are golden and dry.

124
00:11:57,946 --> 00:12:01,671
The great flood will not come for some months yet.

125
00:12:01,671 --> 00:12:07,408
But up in the sky, the stars turn slowly, keeping their ancient appointments.

126
00:12:07,408 --> 00:12:19,701
And one day, before dawn, Sopdet will rise again in the east, and the river will begin to swell, just as it has done every year, for longer than anyone can remember.

127
00:12:29,001 --> 00:12:33,978
Across the river, on the eastern bank, stands a great temple.

128
00:12:33,978 --> 00:12:41,157
Its walls are enormous, built of sandstone blocks, carved with scenes of gods and kings.

129
00:12:41,157 --> 00:12:53,150
In front of it rises a huge gateway with two sloping towers, and before the gateway, tall flagpoles and stone obelisks point up into the night sky.

130
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:59,807
During the day, ordinary people could not go very far into the temple.

131
00:12:59,807 --> 00:13:03,481
The deepest rooms were reserved for the priests.

132
00:13:03,481 --> 00:13:06,083
But at night, it is quieter still.

133
00:13:06,083 --> 00:13:08,762
Most of the priests have gone home.

134
00:13:08,762 --> 00:13:16,338
A few remain, to watch over the sanctuary, and to perform the rituals that kept the world in order.

135
00:13:17,638 --> 00:13:27,762
Inside, the great hall is filled with columns, taller than trees, so thick that several people holding hands could not reach around them.

136
00:13:27,762 --> 00:13:37,885
They are carved and painted to look like bundles of papyrus, so that walking through the hall is like walking through a vast stone marsh.

137
00:13:37,885 --> 00:13:45,201
In the lamplight, the painted reeds and flowers on the columns glow faintly, red and blue and gold.

138
00:13:46,501 --> 00:13:55,719
The priests here washed themselves in a sacred lake beside the temple, several times a day, to be pure before the gods.

139
00:13:55,719 --> 00:13:59,825
Tonight, the surface of that lake is perfectly still.

140
00:13:59,825 --> 00:14:08,346
The stars are reflected in it, and the dark shape of the temple walls, and a single lamp burning in a doorway.

141
00:14:09,646 --> 00:14:16,485
Somewhere deep inside, a priest moves quietly through the rooms, carrying a small lamp.

142
00:14:16,485 --> 00:14:19,315
He checks that the doors are closed.

143
00:14:19,315 --> 00:14:21,987
He adjusts an offering of flowers.

144
00:14:21,987 --> 00:14:30,870
He pauses before a carving of the goddess of the sky, her body arched across the whole ceiling, covered in stars.

145
00:14:30,870 --> 00:14:35,822
Then he walks on, his bare feet silent on the cool stone floor.

146
00:14:45,122 --> 00:14:50,731
Far to the north, many days downriver by boat, stand the great pyramids.

147
00:14:50,731 --> 00:14:56,029
By the time our story takes place, they were already incredibly old.

148
00:14:56,029 --> 00:15:03,351
The largest of them, built for a king named Khufu, was already well over a thousand years old.

149
00:15:03,351 --> 00:15:11,687
To the people of this village, the pyramids were as ancient as the oldest castles and cathedrals are to us.

150
00:15:11,687 --> 00:15:12,856
Older, in fact.

151
00:15:14,156 --> 00:15:22,288
Travellers who sailed down the Nile would see them rising on the western horizon, at the edge of the desert.

152
00:15:22,288 --> 00:15:26,203
Huge, smooth, pale shapes, standing against the sky.

153
00:15:26,203 --> 00:15:39,606
In those days, they were still covered in a casing of fine white limestone, polished and smooth, so that they would have shone in the sunlight, and glowed faintly under the moon.

154
00:15:40,906 --> 00:15:46,069
Even the Egyptians themselves visited them as ancient monuments.

155
00:15:46,069 --> 00:15:56,716
Some left notes scratched on the walls of old temples nearby, saying that they had come to admire the work of the kings of long ago.

156
00:15:56,716 --> 00:16:03,008
Tourists, three thousand years ago, marvelling at the past, just as we do now.

157
00:16:04,308 --> 00:16:07,967
Picture them tonight, far away in the darkness.

158
00:16:07,967 --> 00:16:09,992
Silent, enormous, patient.

159
00:16:09,992 --> 00:16:13,106
The moon moving slowly over their faces.

160
00:16:13,106 --> 00:16:17,078
The desert wind brushing softly across their sides.

161
00:16:17,078 --> 00:16:26,110
They have stood there through thousands of nights exactly like this one, and they will stand through thousands more.

162
00:16:35,410 --> 00:16:39,545
The Egyptians told a beautiful story about the night.

163
00:16:39,545 --> 00:16:48,674
They believed that the sun was carried across the sky each day in a boat, sailing over the heavens from east to west.

164
00:16:48,674 --> 00:16:55,228
And each evening, when the sun set behind the western cliffs, the boat did not stop.

165
00:16:55,228 --> 00:16:57,490
It sailed on, into the night.

166
00:16:58,790 --> 00:17:08,216
The story goes that during the hours of darkness, the sun's boat travelled through a hidden world beneath the earth, called the Duat.

167
00:17:08,216 --> 00:17:19,909
The night was divided into twelve hours, and each hour was like a different region of that hidden world, with its own gates and guardians, its own rivers and fields.

168
00:17:19,909 --> 00:17:25,649
The boat passed through them one by one, slowly, steadily, all through the night.

169
00:17:26,949 --> 00:17:38,000
And at the end of the twelfth hour, the boat reached the eastern horizon, and the sun rose again, renewed and shining, to begin a new day.

170
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:42,004
Every sunrise, in this story, was a small rebirth.

171
00:17:42,004 --> 00:17:47,770
The night was not an ending, but a journey, and the morning always came.

172
00:17:49,070 --> 00:17:53,676
The Egyptians also used the stars to tell the time at night.

173
00:17:53,676 --> 00:18:00,893
They watched for particular groups of stars rising, one after another, through the dark hours.

174
00:18:00,893 --> 00:18:12,409
Priests whose job was to keep the temple rituals on time would note which stars had appeared, and so they would know how much of the night had passed.

175
00:18:13,709 --> 00:18:18,551
There is something very comforting in their idea of the night.

176
00:18:18,551 --> 00:18:23,081
That while we sleep, the sun is not gone, only travelling.

177
00:18:23,081 --> 00:18:29,407
That the darkness is a passage, carefully guided, hour by hour, toward the light.

178
00:18:29,407 --> 00:18:36,045
That somewhere beneath the quiet earth, a little boat is sailing on, and all is well.

179
00:18:45,345 --> 00:18:50,125
Back on the roof in the village, the lamps are burning low.

180
00:18:50,125 --> 00:18:51,988
The stories have ended.

181
00:18:51,988 --> 00:18:58,064
The children are all asleep, rolled in light linen sheets on the reed mats.

182
00:18:58,064 --> 00:19:06,166
The grown ups are lying down too, one by one, as the cool night air finally settles over the valley.

183
00:19:07,466 --> 00:19:11,614
Down in the street below, the last door is closed.

184
00:19:11,614 --> 00:19:15,431
A dog barks once, far away, and then is quiet.

185
00:19:15,431 --> 00:19:25,386
The cat finds a warm spot on a wall that is still holding the heat of the day, curls into a circle, and closes its eyes.

186
00:19:26,686 --> 00:19:33,155
Beyond the village, the river flows on in the darkness, wide and slow and silent.

187
00:19:33,155 --> 00:19:37,868
The papyrus reeds whisper as the breeze moves through them.

188
00:19:37,868 --> 00:19:41,222
A heron stands motionless in the shallows.

189
00:19:41,222 --> 00:19:49,129
The little boats are pulled up on the bank, their sails folded, rocking very slightly on the water.

190
00:19:50,429 --> 00:19:53,100
Above it all, the sky is immense.

191
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:59,253
Thousands upon thousands of stars, from one edge of the desert to the other.

192
00:19:59,253 --> 00:20:06,134
The Milky Way stretches across it like a river of light, a mirror of the river below.

193
00:20:06,134 --> 00:20:14,877
The Egyptians saw the sky goddess in that great arch of stars, bending protectively over the sleeping earth.

194
00:20:16,177 --> 00:20:23,136
Somewhere beneath the horizon, the boat of the sun is sailing through the hours of the night.

195
00:20:23,136 --> 00:20:26,353
It will arrive in the east in its own time.

196
00:20:26,353 --> 00:20:29,570
There is nothing you need to do to help it.

197
00:20:29,570 --> 00:20:36,380
You only need to rest, and let the night pass gently, and trust that the morning will come.

198
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:41,523
Let the warm air of the Nile settle around you.

199
00:20:41,523 --> 00:20:44,549
Let the sound of the river carry you.

200
00:20:44,549 --> 00:20:53,381
Let the stars watch over you, as they watched over everyone who ever slept on those flat roofs, so long ago.

201
00:20:54,681 --> 00:20:59,506
Thank you for spending this evening with The Quiet Archive.

202
00:20:59,506 --> 00:21:00,324
Rest well.

203
00:21:00,324 --> 00:21:01,387
Sleep deeply.

204
00:21:01,387 --> 00:21:02,532
And goodnight.
